1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,560
This cashier is busy ninety percent of the

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,960
time. The average wait in her line is nine

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:07,120
minutes. Make her busy ninety five percent

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,200
of the time, and the wait jumps to

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,280
nineteen. Here's why.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,360
It's one of the most useful equations in

7
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,280
engineering, and it starts with a simple

8
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:16,080
question.

9
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:17,520
Start with two numbers.

10
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,000
The first is how fast customers arrive.

11
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,320
Say fifty four people join the line every

12
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:24,560
hour. Engineers call this the arrival

13
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:26,000
rate, lambda.

14
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,080
The second is how fast the cashier can

15
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:30,240
serve them. On average she takes one

16
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:32,240
minute per customer, so she can serve

17
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:33,440
sixty an hour.

18
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:35,600
That's the service rate, mu.

19
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,760
Divide the first by the second and you get

20
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:38,950
the utilization, rho.

21
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,480
Fifty four over sixty is zero point nine.

22
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,480
She's busy ninety percent of the time, and

23
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:45,920
free the other ten.

24
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:47,360
Here's a puzzle.

25
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,840
If she's free ten percent of the time, why

26
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:51,440
is there a line at all?

27
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,280
Because customers don't arrive on a

28
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,760
schedule. If one person showed up exactly

29
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:57,840
every sixty seven seconds, nobody would

30
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:00,430
ever wait. But real customers arrive at

31
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:02,910
random. Sometimes three walk up at once.

32
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:04,910
Sometimes nobody comes for minutes.

33
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:06,590
The bursts build a line.

34
00:01:06,590 --> 00:01:08,910
The quiet gaps are idle time she can never

35
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:09,630
get back.

36
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:12,110
Queueing theory turns this into one

37
00:01:12,110 --> 00:01:14,670
formula. For one cashier, with random

38
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:16,830
arrivals and random service times, the

39
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:20,030
average wait in line is rho over one minus

40
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:22,350
rho, times the average service time.

41
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:24,110
Look at the bottom of that fraction.

42
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:26,670
One minus rho is the spare capacity.

43
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:28,830
It's the share of time she's free.

44
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:31,310
At ninety percent busy, the spare capacity

45
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:32,590
is zero point one.

46
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:35,390
Nine tenths divided by one tenth is nine.

47
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:37,390
Times one minute, that's a nine minute

48
00:01:37,390 --> 00:01:38,020
wait.

49
00:01:38,030 --> 00:01:40,430
Now run the same formula for other levels

50
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:43,070
of busy. At fifty percent busy, the wait

51
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:43,727
is one minute.

52
00:01:43,737 --> 00:01:45,900
Seventy five percent gives three minutes.

53
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:47,950
Eighty percent gives four.

54
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:49,870
Ninety percent, nine minutes.

55
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:52,350
Ninety five percent, nineteen minutes.

56
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:54,110
And at ninety nine percent busy, the

57
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,430
average wait is ninety nine minutes.

58
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:58,510
Plot it and you get this curve.

59
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:00,670
For a long time it barely rises.

60
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:02,780
Going from zero to seventy percent busy

61
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:04,540
adds only a little over two minutes of

62
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:07,180
waiting. Then the curve bends upward, and

63
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:09,260
past ninety percent it shoots almost

64
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:11,580
straight up. It looks like a hockey stick.

65
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:13,580
Every extra percent of busy costs more

66
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,340
waiting than the one before.

67
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,500
So why does going from ninety to ninety

68
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:19,340
five percent double the wait?

69
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:21,340
Look at the spare capacity again.

70
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,500
At ninety percent, she's free ten percent

71
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,980
of the time. At ninety five, only five

72
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:28,060
percent. You've cut her spare time in

73
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:29,900
half, and the spare time sits on the

74
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:31,180
bottom of the fraction.

75
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:33,260
Halve the bottom, and you roughly double

76
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:35,980
the answer. Nine minutes becomes nineteen.

77
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:38,460
And what if she's busy one hundred percent

78
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,540
of the time, with customers arriving

79
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:42,780
exactly as fast as she can serve them?

80
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:45,420
The spare capacity is zero, and you'd be

81
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:46,780
dividing by zero.

82
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:49,180
In practice, the line never settles down.

83
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:51,420
Every random burst adds people, and

84
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:53,260
there's no quiet time to clear them.

85
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,820
On average, the line just keeps growing.

86
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:57,820
Notice what causes all this.

87
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:00,370
Randomness. A more general version, called

88
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:02,850
Kingman's formula, adds a variability

89
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:05,010
factor. It measures how unpredictable the

90
00:03:05,010 --> 00:03:06,770
arrivals and service times are.

91
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:09,250
Fully random, like our cashier, the factor

92
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:11,410
is one. Make everything perfectly regular,

93
00:03:11,490 --> 00:03:13,570
and it drops to zero, and so does the

94
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:15,650
wait. That's why engineers fight

95
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:18,290
variability. Appointment slots, scheduled

96
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:20,530
deliveries and standard work all shorten a

97
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:22,930
line without hiring anyone.

98
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:25,250
This one curve shapes a lot of design

99
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:27,650
decisions. Call centers use queueing math

100
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:29,490
like this to decide how many agents to

101
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:32,050
staff. Hospitals keep spare beds, because

102
00:03:32,050 --> 00:03:34,130
a full ward means long waits for the next

103
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:36,930
patient. Computer servers slow to a crawl

104
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:38,290
as they near full load.

105
00:03:38,290 --> 00:03:40,370
And in factories, a machine that's almost

106
00:03:40,370 --> 00:03:42,770
always busy piles up unfinished parts in

107
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:43,890
front of it.

108
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:46,050
Three more customers an hour can double

109
00:03:46,050 --> 00:03:47,170
your wait in line.

110
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:49,330
This cashier can serve sixty customers an

111
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:51,890
hour. With thirty arriving each hour, the

112
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:53,490
average wait is one minute.

113
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:55,570
With fifty four, it's nine.

114
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:58,130
Add just three more, to fifty seven, and

115
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:00,290
the wait jumps to nineteen minutes.

116
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:02,220
On the manager's spreadsheet, that's a

117
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:04,940
better day. Less idle time, lower cost per

118
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:07,180
customer. For the people in line, it's

119
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:08,620
twice the wait.

120
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:10,540
So here's what to remember.

121
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,580
Waiting time depends on spare capacity,

122
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:15,340
not on how busy things look.

123
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:18,060
The last few percent of busy are the most

124
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:20,300
expensive. And there are two ways to

125
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:21,420
shorten a line.

126
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:24,700
Add capacity, or remove randomness.

127
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,620
Next time you're stuck in a slow line,

128
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:27,980
look at the cashier.

129
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:30,540
If she never gets a break, now you know

130
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:31,340
why.

131
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,340
If you liked this video, please give it a

132
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:35,180
like and subscribe for more everyday

133
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:36,940
engineering. Thanks for watching!
